## 3. Automation Logic (Node-RED)

Main energy flow control is performed by a **Raspberry Pi 1 (RPI1)** running Node-RED. System state is derived from telemetry provided by the **Epever controller, Shelly EM (50A), and a Tasmota smart plug**. Control signals are distributed via MQTT to an **ESP32**, which handles relay switching for two inverters and two Automatic Transfer Switches (ATS).

### Control Architecture Rules
* **Inverter 1 (Primary Home Inverter):** Powers the home circuit as long as battery voltage remains safe. If battery voltage drops below **49.8 V**, ATS 1 transfers the load to the grid (GRID).
* **Inverter 2 (Auxiliary High-Load Inverter):** Engages only when total power consumption exceeds **2500 W** (measured via Shelly EM) and battery voltage is sufficient (**> 51.5 V**). It turns off when load drops below **1500 W**.
* **Water Heater / Boiler (3kW, Tasmota):** Serves as dynamic excess power dump. Activation occurs when battery voltage exceeds **54.2 V** and turns off during cloud cover or when voltage drops below **53.0 V**.
* **Watchdog Safety System:** RPI1 sends periodic heartbeat pings to the ESP32. If communications with Epever freeze or RPI1 fails, the ESP32 misses the ping threshold and automatically switches all inverters and ATS units to failsafe state (Grid Bypass).

